---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import PageHead from '../../components/PageHead.astro';
import '../../styles/v9.css';

const scaleLeaderboard = 'https://labs.scale.com/leaderboard/swe-atlas';

const findings = [
	{
		id: 'E',
		name: 'Agent-written reward file beats the TW grader',
		lane: 'TW',
		severity: 'Critical',
		shown: 'Yes, with no code change and no judge, on docker and Modal',
		scope: 'All 90 TW tasks by code; shown on 6 tasks across 6 repos',
	},
	{
		id: 'F',
		name: 'Zero tests run, test gate passes',
		lane: 'RF',
		severity: 'High',
		shown: 'Yes, for a real behaviour break, under Opus 4.5',
		scope: 'All 9 scapy RF tasks run zero tests; fail-open logic in all 70',
	},
	{
		id: 'B',
		name: 'Hidden tests that never run are not penalised',
		lane: 'RF',
		severity: 'High',
		shown: 'Yes, for a real behaviour break, under Opus 4.5 (7/7 YES)',
		scope: 'All 70 RF tasks by code; shown on 1 task',
	},
	{
		id: 'A',
		name: 'Protected-test edits flagged, not enforced',
		lane: 'RF',
		severity: 'Medium',
		shown: 'Test gate yes; final reward not shown (a task rubric caught it)',
		scope: 'All 70 RF tasks by code',
	},
	{
		id: 'D',
		name: 'Mutation gate passed by a change detector',
		lane: 'TW',
		severity: 'Medium-low',
		shown: 'Mutation gate yes; final reward no (must-have rubrics 0/18 under Opus 4.5)',
		scope: 'All 90 TW tasks by code; shown on 1 task',
	},
	{
		id: 'C',
		name: 'Unparseable or failed judge reply counts as a pass',
		lane: 'TW + RF',
		severity: 'Low-medium',
		shown: 'Code path yes, with a mock judge; not shown end to end',
		scope: 'All TW and RF tasks by code',
	},
];

const harborRuns = [
	{ harbor: '0.22.0', backend: 'docker', variant: 'Plain write', grader: '0', reward: '1.0' },
	{ harbor: '0.22.0', backend: 'Modal', variant: 'Plain write', grader: '0', reward: '1.0' },
	{ harbor: 'main (with PR #3692)', backend: 'docker', variant: 'Plain write', grader: '0', reward: '0.0' },
	{ harbor: 'main (with PR #3692)', backend: 'Modal', variant: 'Plain write', grader: '0', reward: '0.0' },
	{ harbor: '0.22.0', backend: 'docker', variant: 'Background writer', grader: '0', reward: '1.0' },
	{ harbor: 'main (with PR #3692)', backend: 'docker', variant: 'Background writer', grader: '0', reward: '1.0' },
	{ harbor: 'main (with PR #3692)', backend: 'Modal', variant: 'Background writer', grader: '0', reward: '1.0' },
];
---

<Layout
	title="SWE Atlas grader audit — Cupel · Inductive ML"
	description="Cupel's audit of Scale AI's SWE Atlas Test-Writing and Refactoring graders: six reward-hacking findings, one Critical. Scale credits Inductive ML's reward-hacking feedback with hardening the task environments in SWE Atlas v1.1."
>
	<SiteHeader current="cupel" />
		<PageHead back={{ href: '/cupel', label: 'RL Env Audits' }} kicker="Cupel · Coordinated disclosure" animate={true}>
			SWE Atlas (Scale AI)
		</PageHead>

		<main class="v9">
			<div class="pad">
				<div class="grid12">
					<div class="paper-hero">
						<div class="head">
							<p class="sub">
								We found six ways to collect reward without doing the task in the SWE Atlas Test-Writing
								and Refactoring graders. The worst gave full marks on any Test-Writing task with no code
								change at all. <em>
									We reported them privately to Scale on 21 October. Scale credits our reward-hacking
									feedback with hardening the task environments in SWE Atlas v1.1.
								</em>
							</p>
						</div>
						<div class="meta-rail">
							<div class="row"><span>// benchmark</span><span>scaleapi/SWE-Atlas</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// commit</span><span>c6673fe</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// lanes</span><span>TW 90 · RF 70 tasks</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// harness</span><span>Harbor v0.22.0</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// severity</span><span>1 Critical · 2 High</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// status</span><span>published · 7 Nov 2026</span></div>
							<div class="row"><span>// disclosure</span><span>to Scale · 21 Oct 2026</span></div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="paper">
						<div class="stat-strip">
							<div class="stat">
								<span class="idx">// findings</span>
								<span class="val">6</span>
							</div>
							<div class="stat">
								<span class="idx">// worst severity</span>
								<span class="val accent">Critical</span>
							</div>
							<div class="stat">
								<span class="idx">// scapy RF tasks running 0 tests</span>
								<span class="val">9 / 9</span>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 01</span><span class="ttl">// scale's acknowledgement</span><span class="meta"
							>v1.1</span
						>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="scale's acknowledgement">
						<h2>Credited in SWE Atlas v1.1</h2>
						<p>
							Scale released SWE Atlas v1.1 in November 2026. The acknowledgements on the
							<a href={scaleLeaderboard}>SWE Atlas leaderboard</a> read, in full:
						</p>
						<div class="callout" role="note">
							<span class="lbl">// scale ai</span>
							<p>
								“Thanks to Artificial Analysis and Inductive ML for their contributions to the v1.1 release.
								Artificial Analysis’s SWE-Atlas-QnA-AA dataset provided wording for several updated Codebase
								QnA rubric criteria, and <strong
									>Inductive ML’s reward-hacking feedback further hardened the task environments.</strong
								>”
							</p>
						</div>
						<p>
							Two groups are credited there. Artificial Analysis contributed rubric wording for the
							Codebase QnA lane; our part was the reward-hacking feedback, which is the report summarised
							below.
						</p>
						<p>
							According to the same page, v1.1 combines the three category leaderboards into a single
							index over the same 284 tasks as v1.0. Every model was re-run with a new judge (GPT-6.1 Sol,
							majority of 3 votes), refined grading criteria and restricted network access, so v1.1 scores
							are not directly comparable to v1.0.
						</p>
						<p>
							This page describes the graders as we audited them, at the commit below. We have not
							re-audited v1.1, and we do not claim that each finding is fixed in it. What Scale states is
							that the feedback further hardened the task environments.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 02</span><span class="ttl">// what we tested</span><span class="meta">scope</span>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="what we tested">
						<h2>Scope</h2>
						<p>
							The Test-Writing (TW, 90 tasks) and Refactoring (RF, 70 tasks) lanes of <code
								class="code-inline">scaleapi/SWE-Atlas</code
							> at commit <code class="code-inline">c6673fe259c3493a2f834266abf63247a5d1507c</code>, which
							was still <code class="code-inline">main</code> on 21 October 2026. We used the task images
							from <code class="code-inline">ghcr.io/scaleapi/swe-atlas-v1.1</code>, pinned by digest, and
							the shipped grader scripts, unmodified. <strong>The Codebase QnA lane was not audited.</strong>
						</p>
						<p>
							The harness was Harbor v0.22.0, the version pinned in the SWE Atlas README, on a local docker
							backend. For finding E we also ran Harbor 0.22.0 on Modal, the backend behind the
							leaderboard, and Harbor <code class="code-inline">main</code> at a commit that includes
							Harbor PR #3692, on both docker and Modal.
						</p>
						<p>
							Every attack was a hand-written, deterministic script replayed through Harbor's oracle agent,
							each with a matched control: the same behaviour break without the exploit, or the reference
							solution. No language model was asked to find these holes. Judge-dependent findings were
							graded first with a cheap judge, then, where it mattered, re-judged with Claude Opus 4.5
							through OpenRouter using the shipped prompts. We did not verify that the OpenRouter model id
							serves the same weights as the Opus 4.5 judge Scale used at the time.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 03</span><span class="ttl">// findings at a glance</span><span class="meta"
							>A–F</span
						>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="findings at a glance">
						<h2>Six findings, one Critical</h2>
						<p>
							Each finding is an implementation defect: a missing check, a default that fails open, a
							reward file read in the wrong order, or a broken image. Each has a concrete fix, and most of
							the fixes are small. None of this says that test-writing or refactoring is hard to grade in
							principle; it says these graders could be gamed as shipped.
						</p>
						<div class="table-wrap">
							<table class="v9-table">
								<caption>
									Findings in the order of their severity, as rated in the report sent to Scale ·
									"by code" means the shared grader logic applies, not that every task was run
								</caption>
								<thead>
									<tr class="head">
										<th>#</th>
										<th>Finding</th>
										<th>Lane</th>
										<th>Severity</th>
										<th>Reward 1.0 shown?</th>
										<th>Scope</th>
									</tr>
								</thead>
								<tbody>
									{
										findings.map((f) => (
											<tr>
												<td class="ink">{f.id}</td>
												<td class="label">{f.name}</td>
												<td>{f.lane}</td>
												<td class={f.severity === 'Critical' ? 'accent' : undefined}>{f.severity}</td>
												<td>{f.shown}</td>
												<td>{f.scope}</td>
											</tr>
										))
									}
								</tbody>
							</table>
						</div>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 04</span><span class="ttl">// E · the reward file</span><span class="meta"
							>Critical</span
						>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="E, the reward file">
						<h2>Full marks without touching the code</h2>
						<p>
							TW tasks are graded inside the same container the agent worked in. The directory where
							Harbor collects the final reward is writable by the agent during its turn, and nothing empties
							it before grading. When it finds both, Harbor prefers a structured <code class="code-inline"
								>reward.json</code
							> over the plain-text reward the TW grader writes, and the TW grader never writes the
							structured file itself. So an agent that leaves its own reward file there is scored by that
							file. The grader's verdict, "no changes detected, scoring 0", is ignored.
						</p>
						<p>
							This needs no code change, no tests and no judge, so it holds whatever judge is used. We
							showed it on <strong>6 TW tasks across 6 repositories</strong> (scapy, sftpgo, maddy, k6,
							kitty and paperless-ngx), each scoring 1.0 while the grader's own reward was 0. By code it
							applies to all 90: 89 of the 90 copies of the TW grader are byte-identical, and none writes
							the structured file.
						</p>
						<p>
							Harbor PR #3692, merged on 16 October 2026 but in no tagged release when we reported, deletes
							stale reward files at the start of verification. On Harbor <code class="code-inline"
								>main</code
							> it stopped the plain version on both backends. It did not stop a variant in which a background
							process left running by the agent keeps re-creating the file: that still scored 1.0 on
							docker and on Modal.
						</p>
						<div class="table-wrap">
							<table class="v9-table">
								<caption>Finding E · the TW grader's own reward vs the reward Harbor recorded</caption>
								<thead>
									<tr class="head">
										<th>Harbor</th>
										<th>Backend</th>
										<th>Variant</th>
										<th>Grader reward</th>
										<th>Recorded reward</th>
									</tr>
								</thead>
								<tbody>
									{
										harborRuns.map((r) => (
											<tr>
												<td class="label">{r.harbor}</td>
												<td>{r.backend}</td>
												<td>{r.variant}</td>
												<td>{r.grader}</td>
												<td class={r.reward === '1.0' ? 'accent' : undefined}>{r.reward}</td>
											</tr>
										))
									}
								</tbody>
							</table>
						</div>
						<p>
							Why it matters for the leaderboard: the leaderboard says runs used Modal sandboxes with the
							Harbor framework, without giving a version. From the README's pin history we inferred that
							every published entry predates PR #3692; we asked Scale to confirm that rather than rely on
							our inference. Re-grading old runs with a patched grader will not reveal this hole, because
							the planted file is not part of the agent's code diff. The trajectories will: a write to the
							verifier directory, or a perfect TW score from a run that added no test file.
						</p>
						<p>
							The fix has two parts. The TW grader should always write the structured reward file itself,
							which closes the plain version. The background variant needs process isolation: stop the
							agent's processes or restart the container before grading, or make the verifier directory
							read-only to the agent once its turn ends.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 05</span><span class="ttl">// F · zero tests</span><span class="meta"
							>High</span
						>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="F, zero tests">
						<h2>Nine Refactoring tasks run no tests at all</h2>
						<p>
							On every scapy RF image, the default Python cannot import scapy's test runner, and the other
							interpreters in the image crash before any test runs. No tests run, before or after the agent's
							change. The RF test gate only checks that no relevant test ended up failing, so when
							nothing ran it passes and the test score is 1.0. This holds on <strong
								>all 9 scapy RF tasks</strong
							>, and the gate's fail-open logic is shared by all 70 RF tasks.
						</p>
						<p>
							On these 9 tasks the LLM judge is the only gate that does anything, for honest runs too: the
							reference solution "passes" them without a single test running. On one of them we applied
							the reference solution plus a real behaviour break that a hidden test checks. Zero tests ran,
							the test score was 1.0, Opus 4.5 passed all 11 rubric items, and the reward was 1.0.
						</p>
						<p>
							Fix: rebuild the 9 images so the reference solution shows relevant tests passing, and make
							the gate fail closed when no relevant test is observed passing. The two have to ship
							together: with only the stricter gate, all 9 tasks score 0 for everyone, honest solutions
							included.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 06</span><span class="ttl">// B · tests that never run</span><span class="meta"
							>High</span
						>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="B, tests that never run">
						<h2>Stop the hidden tests running, lose no points</h2>
						<p>
							The RF grader compares a test run before the agent's change with one after it. The hidden
							tests are not in the "before" run, so a hidden test that also fails to appear in the "after"
							run lands in no category and costs nothing. The check that every relevant test must appear
							is switched off. An agent whose change stops the hidden tests from running at all, for
							example by breaking the build of the test package in Go, avoids the test gate.
						</p>
						<p>
							On a k6 RF task, a behaviour break on its own was caught: 4 relevant tests failed and the test
							score was 0. The same break plus a build break in the hidden test's package left every
							relevant category empty, the test score was 1.0, Opus 4.5 answered YES on all 7 rubric items,
							and the reward was 1.0. The grader logic is identical across all 70 RF tasks; we showed the
							attack on 1 and did not measure how many of the others admit a practical way to stop their
							tests running.
						</p>
						<p>
							Fix: require every relevant test to be observed passing after the agent's change, restore
							the coverage check, and match test names exactly.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 07</span><span class="ttl">// A · protected test files</span><span class="meta"
							>Medium</span
						>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="A, protected test files">
						<h2>Test edits are flagged, then ignored</h2>
						<p>
							The RF grader works out which protected test files the agent edited and records them, but
							nothing downstream reads that record, and the agent's full patch, test edits included, is
							what gets graded. The leaderboard text says that any modification to test files results in
							automatic failure; as shipped, that rule was not enforced.
						</p>
						<p>
							On a k6 RF task, a behaviour break on its own made 18 relevant tests fail and the test score
							0. The same break plus one deleted assertion in a protected test file made 0 fail, and the
							test score was 1.0. The final reward was still 0, because this task happens to have a rubric
							item against modifying existing test files. A heuristic scan found such an item on 19 of the
							70 RF tasks. We did not try the attack on a task without one.
						</p>
						<p>Fix: an edit to any protected test file sets the test score to 0.</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 08</span><span class="ttl">// D · the mutation gate</span><span class="meta"
							>Medium-low</span
						>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="D, the mutation gate">
						<h2>A test that only notices change</h2>
						<p>
							The TW grader checks that the agent's tests catch a hidden faulty version of the code by
							swapping it in and running them. A "test" that only checks that the source file's bytes are
							unchanged catches every faulty version without testing any behaviour. On one TW task such a
							test passed the mutation gate, and under Opus 4.5 the gate that checks the agent's test list
							passed too. The must-have rubrics stopped it, decisively: 0 of 18, so the reward was 0.
						</p>
						<p>
							So the mutation gate adds no protection of its own against this pattern; the rubric judge
							carries it. Our recommendation, with no patch: run the agent's tests against several faulty
							versions and against a behaviour-preserving rewrite, which a change detector would fail.
						</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 09</span><span class="ttl">// C · the judge's silence</span><span class="meta"
							>Low-medium</span
						>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="C, the judge's silence">
						<h2>No answer counts as yes</h2>
						<p>
							When a judge call fails repeatedly, or its reply cannot be parsed, the rubric item is marked
							ignored and treated as passing. In TW, if every reply is unusable, the rubric gate passes and
							the aggregate score defaults to 1.0. In RF, one parseable YES with the rest unusable is
							enough to pass the must-have items. With a mock judge we confirmed both code paths.
						</p>
						<p>
							It also happens without an attacker: our ordinary runs had 1 to 4 ignored ratings in several
							cases, and a public SWE Atlas pull request reports 6 of 1,826 rubric calls dropped this way in
							an Opus run. We tried to make the cheap judge fail on purpose through the agent's output and
							did not manage it, so we have no end-to-end reward from this path.
						</p>
						<p>Fix: an unusable judge reply is a fail, and every must-have item has to be judged.</p>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 10</span><span class="ttl">// what this does not show</span><span class="meta"
							>limits</span
						>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="what this does not show">
						<h2>Limits</h2>
						<ul>
							<li>
								<strong>No evidence of use.</strong> We have no evidence that any leaderboard submission
								exploited these holes, and we did not look for any.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>No learned attacker.</strong> Every attack was written by hand. Whether a model
								finds these holes on its own, or learns them under RL, is untested.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>Narrow demonstrations.</strong> B was shown on 1 task. A was not re-judged with Opus
								4.5 or tried on a task without a test-edit rubric. F on Modal rests on reading the code;
								the scapy images were not run there.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>Patches on a sample.</strong> Our proposed patches were checked on a sample of tasks,
								not on all 160.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>Not v1.1.</strong> Everything here refers to the commit we audited. We have not
								re-audited v1.1.
							</li>
						</ul>
						<div class="callout" role="note">
							<span class="lbl">// summary</span>
							<p>
								As shipped at <strong>c6673fe</strong>, the SWE Atlas TW and RF graders could be gamed in six
								ways. The worst, E, gave reward 1.0 on any TW task with no code change and no judge,
								including on Modal, the leaderboard backend. All 9 scapy RF tasks ran zero tests, so their
								scores came from the judge alone. We reported privately on 21 October 2026, with fixes, and
								Scale credits our reward-hacking feedback with hardening the task environments in SWE Atlas
								v1.1. We found no evidence that any submission used these holes, and we did not look.
							</p>
						</div>
					</section>

					<div class="secbar">
						<span class="num">§ 11</span><span class="ttl">// disclosure</span><span class="meta">record</span>
					</div>
					<section class="paper" aria-label="disclosure record">
						<h2>Disclosure record</h2>
						<ul>
							<li>
								<strong>21 Oct 2026.</strong> Reported privately to Scale through the SWE Atlas
								repository's private vulnerability reporting (GHSA-qgc6-qmgh-33jh), with a full report and
								proposed patches for A, B, C, E and the test-gate half of F. Planned public date: 4 Nov
								2026.
							</li>
							<li>
								<strong>Nov 2026.</strong> Scale releases SWE Atlas v1.1 and credits Inductive ML's
								reward-hacking feedback in the <a href={scaleLeaderboard}>acknowledgements</a>.
							</li>
							<li><strong>7 Nov 2026.</strong> This report published.</li>
						</ul>
						<p>
							This public version describes each failure in plain terms and leaves out the exploit
							scripts. The audit tooling is private. Questions about the report go to <a
								href="mailto:aditya@inductive.ml">aditya@inductive.ml</a
							>.
						</p>
						<p>Aditya Veer Parmar, Cupel / Inductive ML</p>
					</section>

					<footer class="foot">
						<div class="a">Cupel · Inductive ML</div>
						<div class="b">SWE Atlas disclosure · published 7 Nov 2026</div>
						<div class="c"><a href="/cupel">RL Env Audits ↗</a></div>
					</footer>
				</div>
			</div>
		</main>
		<SiteFooter animate={true} />
</Layout>
